# Sample .gitignore for tracking / with etckeeper
#
# Place this file at /.gitignore, then run:
#   etckeeper init -d /
#
# The important line is the very first one below: `/*` ignores everything.
# If you're tracking paths by running `git add -f <path>` yourself (the
# easiest approach — see the README's "Choosing a .gitignore strategy"
# section), you're basically done with that line:

/*


# --- Optional: automatic tracking via negation rules ---
# If you'd rather have git pick up new files under certain paths on its
# own (so you don't have to `git add -f` each new file individually), you
# can un-ignore specific paths below with "!" lines instead. This needs more
# upkeep: to un-ignore a nested path, every component of that path must be
# un-ignored.
# Example: to track /home/alice/.bashrc you need three lines:
#   !/home
#   !/home/alice
#   !/home/alice/.bashrc
# Stopping at any level leaves everything deeper ignored.
#
# Un-ignoring a directory tracks its entire contents. To track only selected
# files inside a large directory, ignore its contents first, then carve out
# what you want:
#   !/etc
#   /etc/*
#   !/etc/fstab
#   !/etc/hosts
#
# After init, etckeeper appends its own auto-generated ignore block to this
# file. Your custom rules must appear above that block (put them here).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
